**Read:** [Engineering Thermodynamics — Olivier Cleynen](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Engineering-Thermodynamics-Cleynen.lingua#r_38CM7HKS0GSN5T09YDXFKHRVGQ), liquid response properties; [Static vacuum insulated cryogenic vessels — EIGA 224/20](../sources/Static-Cryogenic-Vessels-EIGA.lingua#r_3RWNED7WZ5GMG7X76ZT96A63HZ), piping and relief context; [Cryogenic vaporisation systems — EIGA 133/22](../sources/Cryogenic-Vaporisation-Systems-EIGA.lingua#r_6CCVCPX8ET2NE6XJ42YW7K4ZDH), complete system boundaries.

Consider a completely liquid-filled, closed volume under a small temperature change. For the liquid, dV/V=αf dT−κT dp. For the enclosure, write dV/V=αv dT+Cp dp, where αv is its effective volumetric thermal expansion and Cp its pressure compliance. Equating them gives dp/dT=(αf−αv)/(κT+Cp), within the linear single-phase elastic assumptions.

For hypothetical αf−αv=0.001 K⁻¹, κT+Cp=10⁻⁹ Pa⁻¹ and ΔT=1 K, the estimate is Δp=1 MPa. The number illustrates sensitivity to small expansion when compressibility is low. Vapor space, dissolution, phase change, plasticity or opening boundaries alter the model.

The boundary is selected by actual valve states and isolated segments, not only by the main vessel. A short retained-liquid segment can have a different thermal and pressure history from a large storage tank.

**Work:** Identify every potentially isolated volume in a conceptual transfer drawing. For each, distinguish a gas inventory, an equilibrium two-phase inventory and a liquid-filled inventory. Explain why their pressure responses require different equations and how the installation's documented provisions address the actual state.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
